WEBVTT


00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:06.660
Getting comfortable with ripping out rows
of knitting is essential for every machine

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.200
knitter.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.150
Has this ever happened to you?

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:13.880
You've found that you've knit too many
rows, or maybe you forgot an increase or a

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:19.850
decrease, or even worse, you look down
and there's a big 'ol knot or slub a yarn

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:21.350
right in the middle of your knitting.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.710
Well, you'll need to rip back a few rows.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.180
In this tutorial we'll talk
about a quick way of ripping.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:33.900
Now this technique works best with
a bit of weight on the knitting.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:39.120
Even if you don't normally knit with
weights, add some before beginning.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:46.380
First remove your yarn from the yarn
feeder and keep the needles in working

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.160
position and the stitches
in the hooks of each needle.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.810
Carefully pull your working yarn taut.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.230
You'll see the current row
of stitches straighten.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.700
Then you'll lift the
working yarn up and back.

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:08.280
This forces the row of stitches below to
pop up and over the fronts of the hooks.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.860
You've removed one row.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.210
Now be sure your stitches
don't slide behind the latches.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.630
Make sure they stay in
the hooks of the needles.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.960
Now repeat this in the opposite direction.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.380
When that's finished,
you've got two rows removed.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:29.100
The trick is to pull and
wiggle, pull and wiggle.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.770
Remove each stitch carefully.

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:39.479
For the machines with gate pegs, take care
that the stitches don't pop up over the

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:41.130
gate peg and get caught.

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:45.190
Plus having weigh on your work will help.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.780
Work slowly so each
stitch forms correctly.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:51.900
Now, here's a tip.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.980
Be careful and count the
number of rows you've removed.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.650
Don't get carried away and lose track.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:04.230
Instead of adjusting your row counter each
time, pull out a needle at the end of your

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.340
needle bed for each row you rip.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:13.810
When you've removed all the rows needed,
change your row counter, return those

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.890
needles back to out of work position
and return to your knitting.

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:23.620
Fair Isle patterns can be
ripped in this fashion.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:28.120
It just takes a little longer because
you'll be pulling two yarns instead of

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:28.660
one.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:34.250
Garter stitch or any stitches that have
been turned can't be ripped as easily this

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:34.700
way.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:41.130
Lace doesn't rip out very nicely either
because of the transferred stitches.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.835
Usually slip and tuck patterns work
pretty well with this fast method.

00:02:49.615 --> 00:02:54.055
Speed ripping on the chunky or the bulky
machines can be a little more difficult

00:02:54.055 --> 00:02:57.295
because of the shape of the
sinker posts, but it can be done.

00:02:59.755 --> 00:03:04.975
I recommend practicing speed ripping on
a swatch before you get into trouble on a

00:03:04.975 --> 00:03:05.575
garment.

00:03:06.355 --> 00:03:09.235
With a little practice, you'll be a pro.
